Thank you very much for the detailed feedback.

**Regarding the raised weaknesses:**

@1. What kind of theoretical result are you interested in? We can add some results for the marginal coverage guarantee for a conformal version of JUCAL. Or we could add a result on asymptotic consistency. Or a result on JUCAL being asymptotically always better or equal than no-calibration, pool-then-cal and cal-then-pool. Please let us know what is unclear to you from a theoretical point of view, so that we can add clarification.

@2. Would you be interested in a proof that increasing c_1, ceteris paribus, increases the aleatoric uncertainty? Or in a proof that increasing c_2,  ceteris paribus, increases the epistemic uncertainty? We can also add some plot where we show epistemic and aleatoric uncertainty for different values of c_1 and c_2 to provide more intuition.

@3. We can add ECE and accuracy. In this case, we will also add a paragraph in the appendix discussing the limitations of ECE and accuracy, which are not strictly proper scoring rules.

@4. Yes, adding image datasets and CNNs to our evaluation would be interesting, while right now we mainly focus on LLMs. Our experimental design massively reduced the CO2 footprint of our paper, as we did not train any models (except the toy models for the plots). Instead, we used the already trained ensemble of LLMs fine-tuned for more than 3k GPU-hours by the authors of a SOTA paper. This allows us to fully isolate our post-processing step with a low carbon footprint. If you want, we can add some experiments for CNNs on MNIST.
Do you know if [1] Roschewitz et al offers a meta dataset too? In other words, are the predictions (e.g., logits) of their already trained ensemble members and the true labels publicly available such that everyone can benchmark their post-processing methods on their already trained models without having to retrain all these models on their own? If that is the case, we would be more than happy to evaluate JUCAl on their metadataset. 

**Regarding the raised questions:**

@1. For neural networks, JUCAL can directly operate on the logit outputs. For models that output normalized probabilities (e.g., certain tree-based methods), we apply the inverse softmax to recover logits before applying JUCAL. We will clarify this in the revised manuscript.


@2. We fully agree. The two are generally not equal, and this non-equivalence is central to our approach: logit diversification would have no effect if they were identical. We will make this motivation more explicit in the revision.

@3. We will improve the readability of Figure 3. We fully agree that our empirical results summarized in line 467 are extraordinarily strong, as you can check in Tables 2-9 in Appendix F. To be more precise, we can write that JUCAL Greedy-50 was never significantly outperformed in any metric by any considered non-JUCAL method, as you can check in Tables 2-9 in Appendix F, where we define "significantly outperformed” via a paired test with alpha=5%”. In other words, across 192 (8 tables, 4 competitors, 6 datasets) comparisons of JUCAL Greedy-50 with other methods, there was not a single comparison where JUCAL Greedy-50 was statistically significantly outperformed. This demonstrated the outstanding robustness and reliability of JUCAL. We will formulate this more precisely in the revision.
Furthermore, JUCAL Greedy-5 is, to the best of our knowledge*, never statistically significantly outperformed by any other considered competitor of ensemble size 5 in any of the metrics.

*For DBPedia JUCAL Greedy-5, in certain metrics, all 4 displayed digits after the decimal sometimes agree with other Greedy-5 competitors. This creates situations where it would mathematically be possible that JUCAL Greedy-5 could be statistically significantly outperformed by a tiny, practically irrelevant margin, which is not reflected in the tables.


@4. We agree that including this discussion would strengthen the paper. We will add a concise section in the main text highlighting the papers’ limitations and future research: Our experiments are limited to text classification. Empirical evaluation on other data modalities, such as, for example, tabular data, image data, graph data, video data, other time series data, and multi-modal data, would be interesting and important future work. As future work on a methodological level, we want to extend JUCAL to LLM-based question answering by combining it with ideas from semantic entropy.
